  "takeaway": "As of Friday, October 9, 2026, U.S. Treasury yields remain elevated but slightly off midweek peaks: the 10Y is around ~5.23% and the 30Y ~5.61% midday. ([archive.ph](https://archive.ph/2026.10.09-142859/https%3A/www.cnbc.com/2026/10/08/stock-market-today-live-updates.html?utm_source=openai)) Japan’s 10Y JGB eased to ~3.03% today after the October 6 auction cleared near ~3.10%. ([fidelity.com](https://www.fidelity.com/news/article/us-markets/202610082118RTRSNEWSCOMBINED_L4N45V01T_1?utm_source=openai)) Brent trades roughly $102–104 and WTI around $90–91 amid ongoing geopolitical risk. ([markets.ft.markitdigital.com](https://markets.ft.markitdigital.com/data/commodities/tearsheet/historical?c=Brent+Crude+Oil&utm_source=openai)) US high‑yield OAS remains contained near ~300–320 bps. ([fred.stlouisfed.org](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/graph/?g=mme9&utm_source=openai)) August CPI printed +3.4% y/y with core at +2.4% y/y; September CPI is due next week. ([bls.gov](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm?WT.qs_osrc=fxb-62236010&utm_source=openai)) Bottom line: Rates stay the dominant pressure driver; today’s pullback hasn’t changed the late‑cycle, fragile regime—stay patient, liquid, and ready for forced‑selling opportunities.",
